Why is this useful (and dangerous)?
A common real-world example of setuid is the `passwd` command — it needs to write to `/etc/shadow` (a root-owned file) even when run by a normal user. Setuid allows this safely in controlled scenarios.

However, if a setuid binary is poorly written or misconfigured, it can be exploited to gain elevated privileges — this is a very common privilege escalation technique in penetration testing and CTFs.

